    "case_id": "121",
    "case_summary": "A 30‐year‐old woman presented with a 2-month history of a swelling in her right cheek associated with severe pain. On examination, there was a diffuse extraoral swelling over the right cheek. Intraorally, a solitary, mobile, 2 cm × 2 cm submucosal mass with ill-defined borders and a smooth mucosal surface was noted on the right buccal mucosa without colour change. Ultrasonography showed a well-defined, hypoechoic lesion in the right cheek region. Based on the clinical and radiographic findings, a provisional diagnosis of an ectopic lymph node versus a lipoma was made.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Angioleiomyoma\nB. Ectopic lymph node was considered\nC. Lipoma was considered\nD. Keloid was included in the differential\nE. Mucocele was included in the differential\nF. Salivary gland tumour (e.g., canalicular adenoma) was included\nG. Neurilemmoma was included in the differential\nH. Neurofibroma was included in the differential\n",
